 ## Root cause
-Unknown — to be established by the diagnosis stage.
+Ten call sites in `lib/apps/fabro-cli/src/commands/{runs/list.rs,model.rs,workflow/list.rs,run/checkpoints.rs}` style secondary/status table cells with `.foreground_color(color_if(use_color, Color::Ansi256(8)))` — an absolute 256-color palette index chosen to *look* dim-grey on a light background — instead of a terminal-relative dim/faint attribute; `cli_table::CellStruct` (via the `Style` trait, already imported at every site) exposes `fn dimmed(self, bool) -> Self` (confirmed at `~/.cargo/registry/.../cli-table-0.5.0/src/style.rs:18,79-82`, which sets `ColorSpec::set_dimmed`, emitting SGR 2 `\x1b[2m` per `termcolor`'s `Ansi::set_color`) for exactly this purpose, and the project already has an equivalent terminal-relative primitive in scope in three of the four files (`Styles::dim` at `lib/foundation/fabro-util/src/terminal.rs:28`, `Style::new().dim().force_styling(use_color)`) that nobody wired into these `cli-table` cell builders. Because `Ansi256(8)` names a fixed color rather than "dim the current foreground," it cannot adapt when the terminal's actual foreground/background contrast is inverted (dark theme), producing near-invisible text — this is a direct, deterministic consequence of the color choice at these ten call sites, not a downstream/derived effect of anything else in the render pipeline (color reaches the output stream correctly, per the confirmed adjacent-cell `\x1b[36m` contrast in repro).
+
+**PROPOSED FIX APPROACH**
+Replace `.foreground_color(color_if(use_color, Color::Ansi256(8)))` with `.dimmed(use_color)` at all ten confirmed sites (`runs/list.rs:128,138,148,185` plus the `status_cell` special case at `178`/`184`; `model.rs:135,138`; `workflow/list.rs:113`; `run/checkpoints.rs:93`). This changes the mechanism from "paint this specific fixed color" to "apply the terminal's own dim/faint SGR attribute," which is exactly what `Styles::dim` already does for non-table text in the same files — so the fix aligns table-cell styling with the pattern the codebase already established, rather than inventing a new one.
+
+For `runs/list.rs::status_cell` (lines 171–186), the `Ansi256(8)` value is also used as a *sentinel* to decide bold-suppression (`.bold(use_color && color != Some(Color::Ansi256(8)))`) and as the default/fallback status color for `Submitted | Pending | Dead`. This needs restructuring, not just a search-replace: introduce an explicit `is_dim` bool (or equivalent) alongside the existing `Option<Color>` return so the bold-suppression and `.dimmed(...)` calls key off that flag directly instead of re-deriving it from a magic color value — removing the `Ansi256(8)` sentinel comparison entirely rather than leaving it as dead logic.
+
+Why this fixes the cause rather than the symptom: the bug is that these sites specify an absolute palette color where a relative attribute belongs; switching to `.dimmed(bool)` removes the absolute color entirely (no `Color::Ansi256(8)` remains anywhere in `lib/apps/fabro-cli/src`), so there is no fixed-color path left to regress to light/dark-specific tuning later. It does not weaken existing behavior: `use_color`/`color_if`-gated suppression for `NO_COLOR`/non-tty output is preserved unchanged (`.dimmed(use_color)` mirrors the existing `color_if(use_color, ...)` gating), and every other (non-`Ansi256(8)`) foreground color at these sites (`Cyan`, status colors like green/red/yellow) is untouched.
+
+Regression test: none of the four affected files currently have tests asserting on emitted ANSI/SGR bytes (confirmed by the reporter's note and by the absence of any `Ansi256` or `dimmed`/SGR assertions in existing `#[cfg(test)]` blocks in these files). A new test should force `use_color = true`, render a row through the real `cli-table` builder (or the smallest slice of it that reaches `.dimmed`/`.foreground_color`), and assert the rendered output contains `\x1b[2m` (or absence of `\x1b[38;5;8m`) for the secondary/status cell. Before the fix, such a test fails (output contains `\x1b[38;5;8m`, no `\x1b[2m`); after the fix it passes.
 
